<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Département des Forêts</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1795">1795</a>–<a href="/years/1814">1814</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">Département</a> des Forêts was a department of the French Republic and Empire from 1795 to 1814, formed from most of the former Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a> after its annexation by revolutionary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Its capital was the city of Luxembourg, and it was named after the forests of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardennes">Ardennes</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s fall the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> turned most of its territory into the Grand Duchy of Luxembourg, joined in personal union with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/departement-des-forets.jpg" alt="Département des Forêts" width="320" height="249" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Excommunicato (original version by Naturals) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:For%C3%AAts_departement_(1812).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> lay in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and took in, besides the former Duchy of Luxembourg, small parts of the Duchy of Bouillon. In 1812 it had 246,333 inhabitants and an area of 691,035 hectares, divided into four arrondissements: Luxembourg, Bitburg, Diekirch and Neufchâteau, with cantons such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arlon">Arlon</a>, Echternach, Clervaux, Vianden, Wiltz and Bastogne. Today its territory is shared between the Grand Duchy of Luxembourg, the Belgian Province of Luxembourg and the German state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland-palatinate">Rhineland-Palatinate</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The city and fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a> capitulated to French revolutionary troops on 7 June 1795 after a six-month siege. On 1 October the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-convention">National Convention</a> decreed the annexation of the Austrian Netherlands, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> was formed on 24 October. The old order was swept away: the privileges of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a> were abolished in November 1795, and from June 1796 births, deaths and marriages were recorded by a civil registrar rather than the parishes. In a deeply Catholic society, civil marriage and divorce caused great consternation. Four central commissioners governed the department over the years, all from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> proper.</p><p>Religious persecution, the suppression of religious orders in the city, requisitions, taxes and compulsory military service from 1798 bred wide discontent, which led that year to the Peasants' War, a revolt of the peasantry in the north. Of 14,176 men of the former duchy conscripted into the French Revolutionary Army and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grande-armee">Grande Armée</a>, 9,809 died. In the city the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a> let a middle class emerge, while from 1803 workers had to carry the livret d'ouvrier, a booklet recording their employers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s defeat in 1814, the lands east of the Our and Sauer went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>'s Grand Duchy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-rhine">Lower Rhine</a>, later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine-province">Rhine Province</a>, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgian-revolution">Belgian Revolution</a> the western part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a> became a Belgian province. The arrondissements, cantons and communes introduced by the French still exist in Luxembourg, and the Code civil of 1804 remains in force there; court proceedings are still conducted in French.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Forêts” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Grand Duchy of Luxembourg was created by the Congress of Vienna in 1815 out of the old Duchy of Luxembourg, a province of the Habsburg Netherlands that revolutionary France had annexed in 1795 as the <b>Département des Forêts</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-luxembourg">Grand Duchy of Luxembourg · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
